WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
As millions mark International Holocaust Remembrance Day,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.460
honoring six million Jews murdered by the Nazis,

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.640
AI is helping preserve survivors' voices.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.660
CBS's Tina Kraus shows us how.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.140
My name is Susan Pollock.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.640
Artificial intelligence is helping British Holocaust survivor

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.320
Susan Pollock keep her story alive.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.200
Susan, can you tell me about your childhood in Hungary?

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.640
We were only two children,

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:36.800
my brother and myself. The Testimony 360 project lets students ask an AI version of Palat questions

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.360
about her life. Using virtual reality headsets, they can walk around the places she experienced,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.100
including the Nazi concentration camps where she was taken as a young teenager.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:55.480
This environment that they placed me in in Auschwitz, so inhuman, they wanted to kill us all.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.800
and they practically did.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.360
Polak and other survivors were filmed answering about a thousand questions for the Holocaust

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.880
Educational Trust, enabling the AI to respond to whatever someone asks.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.240
To be able to explore the Holocaust through a lens of a person, this re-humanizes that history.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:22.260
Polak has always visited schools in the UK to tell them about her life and near death at the

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:29.100
hands of the Nazis. But at 95, she can't travel as much. Now the AI is an opportunity to keep

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:36.540
connecting. I'm just overwhelmed with the technology. Remarkable. Educators say it's

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:42.460
a powerful way for the lessons to remain personal, letting Pollock's voice and so many others

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:51.020
resonate for generations to come. Tina Kraus, CBS News, London. Fewer than 200,000 Holocaust

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:56.040
Survivors are still alive worldwide, with most of them living in Israel and right here in the U.S.
